use ../../lib/UiBehavior

export ui-behavior Presence:
    description:
        Presence handles content appearing and disappearing, such as a panel
        opening or a notification going away, including any animation on the
        way in or out. It belongs to the component that shows and hides the
        content.

    state:
        The component says whether the content should be shown. Presence
        decides when it is actually drawn and when it is actually removed, so
        that animations have time to play.

    leaving:
        When the content is hidden, it stays on screen until its leaving
        animation finishes, and is then removed. If it has no leaving
        animation, or the page can't be seen, such as in a background tab, it
        is removed straight away. Content on its way out already counts as
        closed and can't be used, even while it is still visible.

    entering:
        Content can animate as it appears. Whether content that is already
        shown when the page first loads plays its entering animation is up to
        the chosen library.

    interruption:
        If the content is shown again while it is still leaving, it stops
        leaving and stays, without being removed and drawn again. If it is
        hidden again while still entering, it leaves from wherever it got to.

    reporting:
        The component is told when content has finished entering and when it
        has finished leaving and been removed, so it can wait until the
        content is fully in or fully gone before doing something.

    keeping:
        By default, content is not drawn until it is first shown, and is
        removed when it is hidden. The component can instead keep hidden
        content drawn, so it holds on to what's inside, such as a half-filled
        form or a scroll position. It can also have content drawn from the
        start, even before it is first shown. Content that is kept while
        hidden still can't be seen, reached with the keyboard, or read by
        assistive tools.

    reducedMotion:
        Nothing depends on an animation running. When the user has asked their
        device for less motion, content can enter and leave with little or no
        animation, at the same moments as it otherwise would.

    nonGoals:
        - It doesn't define the animations. How content moves, fades, or grows
            is up to the chosen library and the app
        - It doesn't decide when content should be shown. That's up to the
            component, often through Disclosure
